
  [1mThe swap point.[0m When [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m becomes a real [38;5;153mfetch[39m, the server's id replaces the default at exactly one line
  inside [38;5;153mlogin()[39m. Nothing else in the app moves. That was the point of routing everything through the module.

  [1mError handling.[0m Three cases, all in [38;5;153msession.js[39m:

  - [3m[38;5;153msessionStorage[39m unavailable[0m — it throws in Safari private mode and when cookies are blocked, and a login form is
    exactly where that bites. [38;5;153msetUser[39m wraps the write in [38;5;153mtry/catch[39m, warns on the console, and lets login succeed anyway.
    Persistence is a nice-to-have here; blocking login because storage is disabled would be a worse bug than the one
    we're fixing.
  - [3mMalformed stored JSON[0m — [38;5;153mgetUser()[39m wraps [38;5;153mJSON.parse[39m in [38;5;153mtry/catch[39m and returns [38;5;153mnull[39m on garbage rather than throwing
    into whatever form called it. A caller that gets [38;5;153mnull[39m behaves as it would for a logged-out user, which is the safe
    reading.
  - [3mMissing key[0m — [38;5;153mgetUser()[39m returns [38;5;153mnull[39m. Not an error; it's the ordinary "nobody logged in yet" state.

  [1mOne thing I want to flag rather than silently build.[0m This stores a user identifier where any script on the page can
  read it, and [38;5;153mlogin()[39m still accepts a password it does nothing with. Neither is new — the stub already logs the
  username and never authenticates — and neither is in scope for what you asked. But if this app ever gets real auth,
  the session record is the wrong place to keep anything sensitive: it should hold an id and a display name only, never
  a token or the password. The design above sticks to that, and I'd want it written into the spec as a constraint so a
  later change doesn't quietly start stuffing a token in there.

